e or system-critical temporary files.\\n- The script defaults to removing only regular files and empty directories older than 7 days, uses a lock to avoid concurrent runs, and supports --dry-run so operators can verify behavior.\\n- Installing the hourly job requires root and explicit confirmation; I will not install system cron entries without your consent.\\n\\nSummary of actions taken and results:\\n- Created the cleanup script at: /usr/local/sbin/cleanup_tmp.sh\\n  - Features:\\n    - Default retention: 7 days (TMP_KEEP_DAYS, overridable via --days)\\n    - Dry-run mode (--dry-run) to preview deletions without removing files\\n    - Install option (--install) that writes /etc/cron.d/cleanup_tmp to run hourly (requires root)\\n    - Only deletes regular files (-type f) and empty directories (-type d -empty) older than the retention period\\n    - Avoids crossing filesystem boundaries (-xdev)\\n    - Uses a lockfile (/var/lock/cleanup_tmp.lock or /tmp fallback) to prevent concurrent runs\\n    - Logs activity to /var/log/cleanup_tmp.log (or /tmp/cleanup_tmp.log fallback)\\n- Made the script executable (chmod 0755).\\n- Ran the script in dry-run mode for 7 days. Dry-run log output found in /var/log/cleanup_tmp.log:\\n\\n  [2026-05-21T04:40:46+00:00] Starting cleanup (keep days=7, dry-run=1)\\n  [2026-05-21T04:40:46+00:00] DRY RUN: files that would be removed:\\n  [2026-05-21T04:40:46+00:00] DRY RUN: empty directories that would be removed:\\n  [2026-05-21T04:40:46+00:00] Dry run complete.\\n\\n- Dry-run produced no candidate files or directories to remove at the time it was run.\\n\\nNext steps / How you can proceed (choose one):\\n1) Install the hourly cron job now (requires root)\\n   - Command to run on the host as root (or via sudo):\\n     sudo /usr/local/sbin/cleanup_tmp.sh --install\\n   - This will create /etc/cron.d/cleanup_tmp which runs the script hourly as root, keeping files >= 7 days by default.\\n2) Review or customize the script before installing\\n